{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\n`infra/terraform/bedrock-spend-cap/handler.py` prices CloudWatch token counts\nusing a rate table derived daily from Cost Explorer and matched to models by\nstring heuristics. That layer produced four defects in the twelve days after\ndeployment (2026-09-12), all one class — matching model identifiers against\nbilling labels by substring and suffix:\n\n1. `_rate_for`/`_match`: `input-token-count` also matches\n   `...-cache-write-input-token-count`; longest match wins, so input resolves to\n   cache-write for version-suffix-free ModelIds.\n2. `_normalise`: `Nova2.0Lite` vs `us.amazon.nova-2-lite-v1:0` — neither contains\n   the other, so the model drops to `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`.\n3. `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES` (3083-ada3-3a0f-4051) omits five live services; the CE\n   signal sees about a third of real spend.\n4. `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES` is CamelCase-only; snake_case and `_Global` are\n   skipped.\n\nTwo reached production unalarmed, one for seven days. Four shapes in twelve days\nis why this story deletes the class rather than the instances.\n\n## Approach\n\nPrice every token at a ceiling rate for its direction, set above the highest\nper-direction rate observed on this account, so the estimate is an upper bound and\nthe cap cannot fire late. Per UTC day:\n\n    spend = max( sum over regions and the four token metrics of\n                   undimensioned CloudWatch Sum * CEILING[metric],\n                 ce_spend )\n\n`AWS/Bedrock` publishes a zero-dimension series per token metric, requested by\nsending `Dimensions: []` (omitting the key is not equivalent). Over 2026-09-14 to\n09-24 it equalled the per-ModelId sum exactly, 930,831,660 tokens both ways;\nre-verify before relying on it. The estimate reads exactly those four aggregates\nper region and never adds per-ModelId series — today's handler reads only\nper-ModelId series because mixing the two double-counts.\n\nCost Explorer stays in enforcement: it is the only signal covering Bedrock spend\npublishing no token metric (Cohere Rerank is live; provisioned throughput and\nbatch are structural), and it is orthogonal to all four defects.\n\n**Release discriminator.** An all-empty CloudWatch read is ambiguous: it is both a\nmetrics gap and the normal idle/new-UTC-day state the automatic release depends\non. CE cannot disambiguate it, since CE lags 8-24h and reads zero for hours into a\nbusy day. Instead the handler persists the day's highest observed token total,\nkeyed by UTC date so a new day starts absent. An empty read may release only when\ntoday's key is present and zero-valued; an absent key with a deny attached holds\nthe deny, which also covers a mid-day deploy over old state. A partial-region\nfailure holds the deny, since the surviving region is a floor. The mark governs\nrelease only: on the trip side an empty read yields a zero estimate and CE remains\nthe other arm of the max(), so a gap cannot manufacture a trip.\n\n**Accepted limitation.** Ceiling pricing over-estimates for models far cheaper\nthan the priciest — the cheapest here are two orders of magnitude below, and two\nbill nothing for cache-write, so the factor is unbounded. A large embedding or\nsmall-model batch job can trip the cap at a fraction of intended spend. Accepted\nfor simplicity; the remedy is an exact-match `{ModelId: rate}` override defaulting\nto the ceiling, equality only. Documenting this, the recorded fixtures and the\ndrift guard are the dependent story be3d-712b-c1ac-442b, which also re-adds the\n`cloudwatch:ListMetrics` grant dropped here, since its attribution needs it.\n\n## Figures\n\nMachine-local policy bars spend totals and bill figures from the tracker, because\nthe tickets branch auto-pushes to the public GitHub mirror. Every input is\nderivable, so no executor depends on a private note:\n\n- intended daily cap: the value already configured as `var.bedrock_daily_cap_usd`,\n  unchanged here.\n- `CEILING[d]`: 1.25x the highest per-token rate for direction `d` across all\n  Bedrock services in the trailing 60 days of Cost Explorer.\n- threshold: the intended daily cap scaled by the median of\n  (undimensioned tokens x CEILING) / (CE Bedrock total) over those UTC days in the\n  same window whose CE Bedrock total exceeds one twentieth of the intended daily\n  cap, excluding near-idle days where the ratio is noise.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The seven pricing functions (`_refresh_rates`, `_rate_for`, `_seed_for`,\n  `_normalise`, `_strip_billing_region`, `_marketplace_rate_key`,\n  `_rate_key_for_ce_group`) and six constants (`SEED_RATES`, `RATE_OVERRIDES`,\n  `BEDROCK_SERVICE_VALUES`, `MARKETPLACE_TOKEN_SUFFIXES`, `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K`,\n  `MARKETPLACE_TOKENS_PER_RATE_UNIT`) are absent from the module.\n- [ ] The `TOKEN_METRICS` direction multipliers are removed.\n- [ ] The imports left unreferenced by those removals — `re` and `typing.Literal`\n  — are removed. Checked: `make lint` flags an unused import.\n- [ ] The module docstring no longer describes a derived rate table, seed rates,\n  `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K` or a daily rate refresh.\n- [ ] `_metered_spend` issues exactly four undimensioned `GetMetricData` queries\n  per region, each sending `Dimensions: []`.\n- [ ] `_metered_spend` calls no `list_metrics`.\n- [ ] The `cloudwatch:ListMetrics` action is removed from the execution role's\n  `ReadSpendSignals` statement, leaving `ce:GetCostAndUsage` and\n  `cloudwatch:GetMetricData`.\n- [ ] A `CEILING` mapping gives one rate per token metric, each at least the\n  highest rate for that direction over the trailing 60 days of Cost Explorer, with\n  the derivation recorded as a comment beside it.\n- [ ] Enforcement compares `max(ceiling_estimate, ce_spend)`. Checked: a test\n  driving each arm larger in turn.\n- [ ] `_ce_spend_today` classifies Bedrock spend by predicate (service equals\n  `Amazon Bedrock` or ends with the Bedrock Edition suffix), not an allowlist.\n  Checked: a test over a stub carrying all ten live services, including the five\n  the allowlist omitted.\n- [ ] The day's high-water token total is persisted keyed by UTC date.\n- [ ] An empty read with today's key present and zero-valued may release. Checked:\n  a test on a fresh day.\n- [ ] An empty read after a nonzero reading that day holds the deny. Checked: a\n  test seeding the mark.\n- [ ] An empty read with today's key absent and a deny attached holds the deny.\n  Checked: a test loading pre-change state mid-day.\n- [ ] A partial-region failure holds the deny. Checked: a test raising in one\n  region while the other returns data below threshold.\n- [ ] Any exception from a metered or CE read fails closed. Checked: a test\n  raising from each client in turn.\n- [ ] `infra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf` carries the threshold and warning\n  values derived above.\n- [ ] The `SEED_RATES`, `RATE_OVERRIDES`, `UNKNOWN_RATE_PER_1K` and\n  `RATE_WINDOW_DAYS` environment entries are deleted, along with whichever\n  `variable` or `locals` blocks are left unreferenced. Checked: `terraform plan`\n  shows exactly those deletions, the IAM action removal, and the threshold and\n  warning changes.\n- [ ] No unused Terraform declarations remain. Checked: `terraform validate`.\n- [ ] `state[\"rates\"]`, `state[\"rates_day\"]`, `metered_estimated`, `MeteredRate`\n  and its `RateSource` alias, and the `\" (estimated)\"` rendering are removed.\n\n## Testing\n\nEvery criterion is proved by\n`env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest tests/infra/test_bedrock_spend_cap_handler.py -q`,\nexcept the Terraform ones, proved via\n`python3 scripts/terraform_prod.py plan --out <scratch>/plan.tfplan` and\n`terraform validate`, and the unused-import one, proved by `make lint`.\n`make lint`/`make typecheck` gate commits; `make verify` runs before push.\n\n## Rollback\n\nSet `DRY_RUN=true` to halt attach and detach while keeping the function\nobservable. Full back-out: revert the Gerrit change and re-run the mediated plan\nand apply; Terraform packages the handler by content hash, so the revert restores\nprior code, env and IAM exactly."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1790298159457315111,"uuid":"a62d8321-9ec3-4400-94b3-8a2532173855"}